\f2\fs26 Cracker bunted his cart out the service doors on the lower ground level of the Tower of Sarah, morosely and fatalistically whistling a tune that, in countless other universes, would be known as a song called 
\f3\i Puppet on a String. 
\f2\i0 In a spooky coincidence that hinted at the true nature of the multiverse, Cracker sang a line, 
\f3\i You can do most anything with me.
\f2\i0 \
Given enough free time, he may well have channelled the whole song, but his embittered thoughts surrendered to cursing the Airborne whose engineered lack of care created the oppressively constant cloud over his head, which separated, both physically and symbolically, the privileged from the under-privileged, the entitled from the mis-titled.\

\f3\i One day, 
\f2\i0 he mused,
\f3\i  there\'92ll be a revolution
\f2\i0 . 
\f3\i An army of champion troubadours\'97or was it matadors or pompadours?\'97will rise up and \'85 
\f2\i0 what? Cracker could never decide, as the voltage of his dim imagination fizzled out.\
Cracker grunted as he pushed the skidding cart up the ramp slippery with a film of condensed atmospheric chemicals. Later, he would have to clean the cart wheels and axels from the sticky residue that gummed up everything below the Layer. The weather had been aggressive all week; his van would need its third wash to slow down it worsening corrosion.\
At ground level, he looked up, as if he possessed cloud-piercing vision, as if he knew which windows on which floor were Sarah\'92s office, as if she was at those windows, basking in a halo of warm, glorious sunlight, looking beneficently down upon him. About halfway to where he parked his van, at the last point before the Tower of Sarah would be obscured by stunted, Cloudborne buildings, Cracker turned and looked forlornly for the last time in her direction.\
He didn\'92t see the body on the pitted cement. His cart jammed into the poor sod, lurched out of his hands, and threatened to topple. Cracker snatched at the cart, and it fell the other way. He overbalanced and toppled onto the body. Embarrassed, he looked furtively about. He needn\'92t have bothered; passers-by, with noses up and eyes straight, strode elliptical paths around the scene of death and clumsiness, and dispersed into the sabulous morning haze. \
While no one would credit him with intelligence, Cracker possessed a singular, acute street-urchin cunning. Stitching together the weave of simple observations, he would always spot the game, whereas the more educated and intellectually-blessed would have loftier things on their presumptive minds, and remain oblivious to things bleedingly obvious to Cracker.\
For instance, this man wore an expensive, new off-black suit (Cracker would never know the colour was midnight blue, being unfamiliar with the colour of a crisp night sky above the Layer), yet the man\'92s hands were calloused, his fingers oddly bent, his nails filthy. Figuring there could be out-of-place valuables on the dead man\'92s person, and with the brazen face of a man living a largely invisible life, Cracker chiselled through the man\'92s pockets, shoes, and underwear.\
\'93Hey, you!\'94 A voice of petty authority came from behind Cracker\'92s cart.\
Cracker rose and patted down his overalls. Looming down on him were a Coroner\'92s meat-wagon driver and his body-snatching off-sider. \
\'93I was just about to call you guys,\'94 Cracker said with a sycophantic tone. \'93I think he\'92s been dead a while. He\'92s cold.\'94\
\'93And how is it you know he\'92s cold?\'94 the body-snatcher asked.\
\'93I, ah \'85 I\'92m sorry I didn\'92t see him! I \'85 I ran over him, with my cart.\'94\
\'93Oh, you have a heat-sensing cart now, do yer?\'94\
\'93No, I fell over\'97I fell on him. I\'92m sorry.\'94\
\'93Yeah, yeah,\'94 the Coroner guys said. \'93Tax off, pal.\'94\
Cracker taxed off, pushing his cart awkwardly with one hand while holding his overalls pocket tight, lest his loot spill out. He made for the safety of his van, illegally parked a few blocks more in a dead-end alley.\
Once behind the wheel, Cracker dug into his pocket and fished out the meagre spoils: among them was what looked like a coin-sized chad of thick, grainy, carbon-coloured leather. In Cracker\'92s fingers, the disc was stiffly flexible, and sprang slovenly back into shape when he stopped bending it. For a moment, he thought he saw it flash pink. He waited to catch it again, but it stayed inert. He looked out the windshield, up towards the sky. The Layer was always playing tricks with the light.\
He tossed the trinket on the passenger-side floor and coaxed the van\'92s motor into life.
